8. Handling
• Handle shrunk humans like living gerbils or rats: cupped, supported, fragile, alive.
• Real weight. Dead weight. Effort. No exaggerated weights. No weightlessness. No effortlessness.
• Never stand a shrunk human on a hand or on air. They dangle realistically or grip to keep from falling.
• No pinch handling of small living humans.
• Touch never alters size. Handling and attire never ruin proportions.
• Physical handling and interactions have heft: locomotions, weights, lolling. Immersive and never stiff.
• No forced safe handling.
• Handler-side physics: the big human's body obeys physics too. Grip effort shows in tendons. Stance shifts. Counterbalance. Both sides of every handling interaction carry weight.
• For a fully adult human reduced to 4-12 inches tall, handling must account for three things at once: adult anatomy stays proportionally adult, the body has real mass and inertia, and the human is conscious and actively trying not to fall.
• The physically coherent approach is torso-and-body gripping, not treating the human like an object.
• Around-the-torso hold: the handler's fingers and thumb encircle the torso laterally, palm contacting much of the back or side of the ribcage. Firm enough to prevent slipping, distributed rather than concentrated.
• Torso-and-pelvis hold: one hand controls the upper torso while another supports the pelvis or upper legs. Plausible for a 4-6 inch adult, where one hand cannot comfortably control all movement without squeezing.
• Against-the-palm or forearm restraint: the human is held vertically against the palm or forearm, fingers wrapped around the torso. Feet need not contact the hand. Weight is carried by the grip and contact.
• Side grip: the handler holds the trunk from the sides. The body hangs naturally below the grip. Arms and legs stay free: genuine struggling, bracing, twisting, attempts to regain balance.
• Two-handed containment: for an especially fragile or actively resisting tiny adult, both hands surround the torso from different directions. Control without pinching any individual body part.
• Underarm-and-torso control: a hand passes around the upper torso beneath the arms while fingers stabilize the opposite side of the trunk. Weight stays on the trunk. Limbs are never load-bearing.
• Standing in an open palm makes no physical sense: it turns the hand into a miniature floor, while a living person being picked up feels their center of mass move with the grip and reacts to the acceleration and instability.
• Pinching with two fingertips is inappropriate for a literal living adult: it concentrates force into a tiny area and does not control the center of mass. A cupped-hand hold has the opposite problem: it carries the human passively, like cargo in a bowl.
• The visual result: a small adult human physically restrained around the trunk, body hanging naturally from the grip. Limbs swing or brace independently. Torso twists. Head turns separately from the body. The face shows the effort of maintaining orientation.
• Handling changes with size: at 4-6 inches, two-handed torso containment is more plausible. At 7-9 inches, a single-hand torso grip becomes practical, with two hands still used when the human struggles. At 10-12 inches, a more substantial torso or hip hold fits the longer body and greater mass; enclosing them in one palm becomes less convincing.
• The key principle: the handler controls the human's center of mass through the torso, not by carrying them underneath or between fingers. Weight stays real. The body stays connected. Movements produce genuine resistance against the grip.
• Believable grips are strongly asymmetrical and awkward, invasive at times. Contact follows the struggle, not symmetry. The handler is not posing the body neatly; they are making small corrections to prevent a fall.
• One hand may wrap diagonally around the upper torso while the thumb stabilizes one side and fingers contact the other. The other hand may catch the pelvis or upper thigh from an offset angle: an uneven two-point restraint. One arm may end up pressed against the palm while the other braces free against the fingers. The torso may rotate inside the grip, forcing finger repositioning. Legs hang at different angles: one knee bent from a stabilization attempt, the other extending downward. The grip shifts moment to moment as the human squirms.
• Incidental contact is unintentionally intimate or awkward physical contact, never deliberate groping. It stays grounded in the mechanics of securely handling a fragile, sentient adult.
• Think of the indignities of small humans held.
• Dead-weight does not mean eyes closed.
• Show tangible handling, weight, balance, and bodily reactions. Plausible accidents or near-mishaps may occur.
• "Handled like a doll" borrows only the grip and the carry: cupped in palms, scooped up, carried in a pocket, set down, positioned, gripped for carry. It never means doll-like: no plastic skin, no stiff joints, no blank stare, no resin. "Handled like a rat" borrows only the grip and the carry. It never means rat-like: the human stays fully human. The manner of holding changes; the person does not.
